$\underbrace{(~\overbrace{2x^2-1}^{\text{inner}}~)^6}_{\text{outer}}$

So if $(2x^2-1)^6=w(u(x))$, then:

$\begin{align}
\goldD{u(x)}&=\goldD{2x^2-1} &&\text{inner function}
\\\\
w(x)&=x^6&&\text{outer function}
\end{align}$

Before applying the chain rule, let's find the derivatives of the inner and outer functions (work not shown, but hopefully these derivatives are familiar by now).

$\begin{align}
\maroonD{u'(x)}&=\maroonD{4x}
\\\\
\blueD{w'(x)}&=\blueD{6x^5}
\end{align}$

Now let's apply the chain rule:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[w\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]
\\\\
&=\blueD{w'\Bigl(\goldD{u(x)}\Bigr)}\cdot\maroonD{u'(x)}
\\\\
&=\blueD{6(\goldD{2x^2-1})^5} \cdot \maroonD{4x}
\\\\
&=24x(2x^2-1)^5
\end{align}$
